{# Four things somebody checks before booking, and nothing else.

   The time zone is a span, not a value. 87 of the 93 countries we sell to sit
   in one, and the other six do not: Russia runs GMT+3 to GMT+10 in this data,
   the United States GMT-10 to GMT-4. Printing one offset for those is not a
   rounding error, it is the wrong answer, so a country with more than one says
   so and a country with one says it plainly.

   Both country codes, because both get used and neither is guessable from the
   other: the two-letter one is what the page's own address is spelled with and
   what a form asks for, the three-letter one is what a passport and a boarding
   pass carry. Four tiles is what the grid holds, so they share one.

   No currency and no visa rule, which the reference shows and this database
   does not hold. Both would be seed data rather than a query, and inventing
   either on a page somebody books a flight from is worse than leaving it out. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        { 'icon': 'fa-passport', 'value': place.code ~ ' · ' ~ place.code_iso_3, 'label': 'Country code' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-city', 'value': place.cities, 'label': place.cities > 1 ? 'Cities' : 'City' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-plane-departure', 'value': place.airports, 'label': place.airports > 1 ? 'Airports' : 'Airport' },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-clock',
            'value': place.timezone_min == place.timezone_max
                ? gmt_offset(place.timezone_min)
                : gmt_offset(place.timezone_min) ~ ' – ' ~ gmt_offset(place.timezone_max),
            'label': place.timezone_min == place.timezone_max ? 'Time zone' : 'Time zones'
        }
    ]
} only %}
